  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/sperata-acicularis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Catfish of the genus Sperata can be easily identified by the black spot in the adipose fin. Like all species of Sperata, this catfish is a giant. The largest specimen reported so far was 180 cm long, but the usual size is 30–40 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/sperata-acicularis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Determining the four currently accepted species of Sperata is tricky. Only S. seenghala, a widespread species from India, can be comparatively easily identified, because in that species the eye is positioned in the first third of the skull, while in the other species it is at about half of the skull length. Bony structures of the skull and the foreback must be compared to identify the three remaining species, which is impossible to do in live juveniles.</p><p>Sperata acicularis was imported, or at least the fish were obtained under that name. The supplier is very reliable and S. acicularis is the only species of the genus reported so far for Burma, so there is no reason to doubt the identification.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/sperata-acicularis-2/",
      "html": "<p>S. acicularis is the only species of the genus reported so far for Burma (Myanmar).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/sperata-acicularis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Species of Sperata spawn in pits in the ground and guard eggs and fry.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/sperata-acicularis-2/",
      "html": "<p>As these catfish are very popular as food fish, there are intentions to keep and breed them in aquaculture, but this has so far failed on a larger scale.</p>"
    }
  ],
